confconsole (2.2.3+keel8) trixie; urgency=low

  * keelfirstboot.py, the first boot screens handbook decision 0020
    asks for, run by the inithooks hooks 75keel-role and 80keel-cloud
    (python3 /usr/lib/confconsole/keelfirstboot.py role|cloud).
  * Role of this node: Standalone (the default), Primary or Replica. A
    primary or a replica then goes through the Overlay network screen's
    own actions (this node's key, its overlay address, its peers) and
    the Database mode screen of its role, loaded the way confconsole
    loads it: no second copy of either. Each step that needs the other
    node says what to do on it and can be left with Later, which names
    the confconsole entry that finishes it. The role reaches the
    description only through the Database mode screen, so a node left
    half way is still standalone. A fresh node that stays standalone
    has database.server.role: standalone recorded, validated by keel,
    and nothing applied.
  * The overlay change is confirmed from the first boot's console when
    the operator says Yes, as the Overlay network screen offers: keel
    accepts a console (inithooks draws on /dev/tty1), and the change
    never moves the uplink (--skip-uplink), so the console cannot be
    the path it breaks. What that does not test, the overlay and SSH
    over the uplink, the screens ask to test from the other node. In
    an LXC container that console is /dev/lxc/tty1, which keel accepts
    from 0.11.2 on: Recommends keel (>= 0.11.2).
  * Database mode: a screen text too long for a form (Primary, Replica)
    is shown first in a message box, which scrolls, and the form says
    only what it is. On a 24 row console (lxc-console, pct console) the
    form left its fields no room and dialog failed with "Can't make
    sub-window", in confconsole as much as at first boot.
  * A form field holds up to 1024 characters, scrolling, whatever its
    width: dialog cut the addresses offered for 'Answer on' at 44, and
    keel refused the cut address.
  * Password boxes are sized to their text: at a fixed 10 rows the
    Replica screen's box drew its field over its frame and lost its
    buttons.
  * The first boot's boxes are no taller than the terminal; confconsole
    draws 25 rows, and an LXC console is often 24.
  * The role menu opens on the role the description holds, Standalone
    when it holds none (confconsole's Console.menu takes default_item).
  * Keel Cloud API key: optional, empty means standalone and Cancel
    changes nothing. A node that holds a key is asked Keep, Replace or
    Remove, and only Remove clears it. The key goes
    to /etc/keel/secrets/cloud_api_key (root, 0600) and the description
    references it as hub.api_key; empty is hub.api_key: skip. The screen
    says in one sentence that the key takes effect once the service
    exists and that nothing contacts any service now. Also in the
    Instance menu as Keel Cloud.
  * Not asked when the description declares database.server.role or
    hub.api_key, or HUB_APIKEY is preseeded (SKIP, or a key, which is
    stored without a screen), when there is no terminal, or without
    keel; keel-init asks again and ignores what was preseeded. The screens draw on the terminal of
    standard input when standard output is not one.
  * Usage screen: no TKLBAM status line and no "TurnKey Backups and
    Cloud Deployment https://hub.turnkeylinux.org" footer.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 08:45:00 +0000

confconsole (2.2.3+keel7) trixie; urgency=low

  * Database mode, Cloud / Primary: 'Allow replication from' no longer
    offers this node's /64. MariaDB matches the text of the replica's
    address, and in fd3d:80b2:d0d7::/64 the replica fd3d:80b2:d0d7::2
    is not written fd3d:80b2:d0d7:0:..., so the grant keel made for the
    /64 refused it (Template B smoke test, 2026-09-30) and keel 0.11.1
    refuses such a prefix. An empty field is prefilled with the overlay
    peers' addresses instead, each replica by its own; with no peer it
    stays empty. The screen and the empty-origin warning ask for each
    replica's address, and the screen says the field offers every
    overlay peer, replica or not.
  * An origin the description already holds that keel refuses on
    MariaDB (a prefix inside a group, one whose zero group the text of
    an address compresses away, a host pattern with ::) is named above
    the form and replaced by the overlay peers' addresses, instead of
    being prefilled for keel to refuse.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 07:40:00 +0000

confconsole (2.2.3+keel6) trixie; urgency=low

  * Instance menu: add Overlay network, the console half of step 2 of
    handbook decision 0020. It shows this node's WireGuard public key
    (made by keel network wireguard key on first use; the private key
    never leaves keel), its overlay address and port, and its peers, and
    adds or removes a peer: the other node's public key, overlay address
    and endpoint. It configures THIS node only.
  * The first address is keel's suggestion, a random unique local /64.
  * Changes are staged, validated by keel, committed and applied with keel
    spec apply --system-only --defer-certificate --skip-uplink, without
    the database screens' --skip-network, so keel brings the overlay up
    under the confirmation window of decision 0018 and never moves the
    uplink from this screen (keel 0.11's --skip-uplink). The screen says
    the change reverts by itself unless confirmed from a new session,
    over the overlay or the uplink, and offers to confirm from the
    console, which keel accepts only from a real console.
  * It says how to confirm whenever a network change waits after apply:
    one this run brought up, even when a later step failed, one whose
    rollback failed too and that keel's timer still reverts, or one an
    earlier run left, which keel names when it refuses another.
  * Recommends: keel (>= 0.11.0), the first keel with --skip-uplink; an
    older one stops the screen's apply with a usage error.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 12:30:00 +0000

confconsole (2.2.3+keel5) trixie; urgency=low

  * Database mode, Cloud / Primary: once the primary is configured, the
    screen shows what each replica's screen asks for: the addresses to
    replicate from, IPv6 first, the port, the replication account keel
    names (repl) and the file that holds its password. A primary that
    answers on loopback only is offered this node's own addresses in the
    form, every stable one and public before unique local (the usage
    screen's single pick per interface was the fd00:: one on a test VM),
    and one nobody can reach says so instead of handing out ::1.
  * Cloud / Primary: when the password file holds nothing, offer to
    generate a password, write it root only (0600, directory 0700) and
    show it once, after the apply, to paste into each replica. A file that
    already holds one is kept and never shown or regenerated, because the
    replicas configured with it would stop authenticating.
  * Cloud / Replica: ask for the replication password itself, hidden, and
    write it to the file the description references, instead of asking the
    operator for a file to create by hand. A blank keeps the file's value.
  * Cloud / Replica: warn before anything is changed that becoming a
    replica replaces the data on this node with a copy of the primary; No
    leaves the description, the secret and the server as they were. keel's
    own refusal is still asked separately before --destroy-local-database.
  * The password is written after keel spec validate accepted the new
    description and before it is committed and applied, so a refused
    description leaves no credential behind and the apply never runs
    without it.
  * The mode screens run apply with --skip-network and --defer-certificate:
    saving a database mode never rewrites the interfaces, never opens the
    network revert window, and never asks for a certificate. The screens
    say so.
  * Primary: 'Allow replication from' is prefilled with this node's /64,
    public before unique local. Left empty, it authorizes nobody (keel
    drops every replication account), and the handout says that first.
    A failed apply still shows a generated password, under a warning that
    the node is not ready.
  * The handout is shown with a textbox read from a file in a private 0700
    directory removed when the box closes, never as a msgbox, whose text
    dialog receives as an argument that anyone on the machine can read.
    The password box runs with that directory as TMPDIR as well.
  * The address to paste is shown bare; a pasted [address] is accepted.
  * Answering No to dropping the local databases puts the description and
    the password file back and applies the old description again, since
    keel had already rewritten the server's configuration. Only an old
    description that declares a database server is applied again; for
    one that does not, the screen says the replica configuration stays
    until a database mode is applied.
  * A description that cannot be committed takes the password written for
    it back with it, so no password is left in a file that no screen will
    ever show again.
  * The handout falls back to a box sized by dialog on a terminal too
    small for it, as every other box of the console does.
  * The secret is written all at once (a new 0600 file replacing the old),
    the staged description gets a unique name, and a fresh appliance with
    no description gets one started instead of an errno.

